# ---- Guide: fire safety for holiday lets in Wales (Welsh Government guest accommodation guide, Sep 2023) ----
WS=[('Welsh Government: A guide to making your guest accommodation safe from fire (Sep 2023, PDF)','https://www.gov.wales/sites/default/files/publications/2023-09/guest-accommodation-safe-from-fire_0.pdf'),
    ('North Wales Fire and Rescue Service: Self-catering holiday accommodation','https://www.northwalesfire.gov.wales/keeping-you-safe/at-your-business/guidance-documents/self-catering-holiday-accommodation/'),
    ('Welsh fire and rescue services: Self-catering holiday accommodation guidance (PDF)','https://www.southwales-fire.gov.uk/app/uploads/2021/05/Final-Self-Catering-Holiday-Accommodation-Guidance_en_web.pdf'),
    ('Regulatory Reform (Fire Safety) Order 2005','https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2005/1541/contents'),
    ('Development of Tourism and Regulation of Visitor Accommodation (Wales) Act 2026, section 9 (fire safety standard)','https://www.legislation.gov.uk/asc/2026/10/section/9/enacted')]
WCK=[('Hazards',['Electrical installation inspected and tested at least every 5 years by a competent electrician','Appliances visually checked between lets, registered with the maker and checked against recall lists','Extension leads only for small appliances under 13 amps in total; no plug-in multi-socket adaptors','Gas appliances checked and serviced every 12 months by a Gas Safe registered engineer','Heating and hot water serviced every year; portable heaters avoided (no radiant bar fires or LPG heaters)','Carbon monoxide alarm in each room with a gas, oil or solid fuel appliance','Log burner or open fire: spark guard, chimney swept at least yearly, metal ash bin, fuel stored away from heat, guest instructions','LPG cooker: cut-off device on the pipework from the outside supply','Deep fat fryer only if thermostatically controlled; extractor filters cleaned regularly','No candles, tea lights or ethanol burners provided, and a policy banning them','Smoking policy set (covering vaping); guests encouraged not to smoke','Bins and combustibles kept clear of the building, especially windows','Petrol and other flammables stored safely, ideally outside in a locked metal bin','Barbecues, patio heaters and fire pits sited well away from the building (never on flat balconies)','Furniture meets the Furniture and Furnishings (Fire) (Safety) Regulations 1988 (check labels)']),
     ('Escape and alarms',['Escape routes (hall, stairs) clear of storage','Exit doors open from inside without a key (latch, thumb turn or lever)','No guest bedroom reached only through another room, unless on the ground floor with a direct exit or escape window','Doors onto the hall and stairs are solid, well-fitting doors (hollow-core, thin-panel or plain-glazed doors replaced with 30-minute fire doors)','Interlinked smoke alarms in hall, landing, stairs, living and dining rooms, and bedrooms','Heat alarm in the kitchen (and utility or laundry room)','Alarms mains powered with a tamper-proof backup battery (Grade D1); sealed long-life battery alarms (Grade F1) only as a short-term measure','Torch in each bedroom, or plug-in night lights on the stairs and hall, if escape lighting is not fitted','Listing says clearly if the property is not suitable for some guests (for example people with limited mobility)']),
     ('Paperwork and guests',['Written fire risk assessment, specific to this property, with an action plan for anything marked "no"','Fire action notice and escape plan for guests (back of bedroom door or welcome pack)','Exact address and postcode (or better location details), and how to call 999, given to guests','Landline considered if mobile signal is weak','Smoke, heat and CO alarms tested at every changeover','Testing and maintenance log kept (paper or digital)','Assessment reviewed at least yearly, and after any significant change or a fire'])]
WFAQ=[('Do holiday lets in Wales need a fire risk assessment?','Yes. The Regulatory Reform (Fire Safety) Order 2005 applies in Wales to any short-term let that is not someone\'s main home, even if you let it only once. You must carry out a suitable and sufficient fire risk assessment and document the findings.'),
      ('Is the Welsh guidance different from the English one?','The law is the same Fire Safety Order, but Wales has its own guide, published by the Welsh Government in September 2023. It covers single ground or ground-and-first-floor premises sleeping up to 10 people with no more than four first-floor bedrooms, plus individual flats. Its principles can also be applied to caravans, glamping pods, shepherds\' huts, lodges and yurts.'),
      ('Can I do the fire risk assessment myself?','For small premises, in most cases yes, without specialist knowledge, by following the Welsh Government guide and its template. If you are not confident, use a competent fire risk assessor. You stay legally responsible either way.'),
      ('What smoke alarms does a holiday let in Wales need?','Interlinked smoke alarms in hallways, corridors, staircases, lounges, dining rooms and bedrooms, and heat alarms in the kitchen and any utility or laundry room. Preferably mains powered with a tamper-proof backup battery (Grade D1). Sealed long-life battery alarms (Grade F1) may be acceptable short term, around 2 to 3 years.'),
      ('Do I need a fire extinguisher in a self-catering let in Wales?','No. The Welsh guide says there is no need to provide firefighting equipment in self-catering premises, because guests should evacuate. If staff are on the premises, equipment should be provided and staff trained. It also says multi-purpose powder extinguishers should not be provided, as they are not suitable in enclosed spaces.'),
      ('Do I need fire doors with self-closers?','Not usually. Solid, well-fitting conventional doors (typically 44mm solid timber or a flaxboard or chipboard core) are normally acceptable. Hollow-core and thin-panel doors and plain glazing will normally need replacing with 30-minute fire doors. Intumescent strips, smoke seals and self-closers are not considered essential in properties the guide covers.'),
      ('Do I need a carbon monoxide alarm?','Yes, the guide says to provide one in each room containing an appliance that runs on gas, oil or solid fuel, such as a boiler or log burner.'),
      ('How often should I test the alarms?','Domestic smoke, heat and carbon monoxide alarms should be tested monthly. In self-catering lets where you are not present, do the weekly and monthly checks at every changeover.'),
      ('Who enforces fire safety in Welsh holiday lets?','Your local fire and rescue service: North Wales, Mid and West Wales, or South Wales. It may inspect your property and can give advice, but it cannot do the risk assessment for you. Failure can lead to enforcement action, closure or prosecution.'),
      ('Will the new Welsh licensing scheme change this?','The Development of Tourism and Regulation of Visitor Accommodation (Wales) Act 2026 sets a fire safety standard for regulated visitor accommodation: keep a written fire risk assessment and a record of the precautions taken, and keep both under review. The licensing scheme it belongs to is not in force yet.')]
